# Quality Assurance

## Code Quality Checklist

### Python Modules (`src/`)

- [ ] Follows PEP 8 style guide.
- [ ] Type hints used for all public function arguments and return types.
- [ ] Docstrings included for all public functions, classes, and modules.
- [ ] No hardcoded paths (use `pathlib` or relative paths).
- [ ] No hardcoded API keys or secrets (use environment variables or `.env`).
- [ ] Meaningful variable names (`agent_config` not `ac`).
- [ ] Comments explain the *why* of architectural decisions, not just the *what*.

### Jupyter Notebooks (`notebooks/`)

- [ ] Focused on a single concept or workflow.
- [ ] Markdown cell before each major code section explaining its purpose.
- [ ] No duplicated logic from `src/` — import modules instead.
- [ ] Clear and descriptive cell outputs.
- [ ] Prefer `Kernel → Restart & Run All` clean runs.

## Agent Implementation Checklist

- [ ] Tool functions have accurate, descriptive names and docstrings.
- [ ] Agent definition, tool registration, and execution are separated.
- [ ] Error handling covers LLM timeouts, tool failures, and unexpected outputs.
- [ ] API keys and credentials loaded from environment variables.
- [ ] Agent actions logged for observability.

## Security Checklist

- [ ] No API keys, tokens, or secrets committed to the repository.
- [ ] `.env` files listed in `.gitignore`.
- [ ] No prompt injection vectors introduced by untrusted input passed to LLMs.
- [ ] External tool calls validated before execution.

## Documentation Quality

- `README.md` must match actual implementation status and scope.
- ADRs in `docs/adr/` document significant architectural decisions.
- Each `src/` subfolder should include a `README.md` explaining its purpose.
